Package: phonActivR
Type: Package
Title: Simulating and Fitting Prosodic Constraints on Lexical Competition
    in Spoken Word Recognition
Version: 0.3.0
Date: 2026-09-30
Authors@R: c(
    person("Yu-Cheng", "Lin", email = "yucheng.lin@utrgv.edu", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-5905-6928")),
    person("Hitomi", "Kambara", role = "aut",
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-5302-1305"))
  )
Description: A pure-R implementation of a compact, bounded lateral-inhibition
    competition model in the interactive-activation family (cf. McClelland &
    Rumelhart, 1981) for generating predictions about phonological competition
    in spoken word recognition. It is not the TRACE model: it omits TRACE's
    feature and phoneme unit levels, its lexicon, time-slice reduplication,
    and top-down feedback, and its parameter defaults are its own. Each competitor receives bottom-up
    input only while the segments it shares with the target unfold, so
    competition rises and then resolves at the disambiguation point.
    Introduces the delta parameter, which operationalizes cross-linguistic
    prosodic processing constraints (e.g., moraic, syllabic) as a gate on
    sub-prosodic competitor input that either delays that input (buffered
    delay) or removes the part of it that arrives before the gate opens
    (signal-locked truncation). Supports item-level simulation
    across full experimental stimulus sets with automatic phonological overlap
    computation from a binary adaptation, constructed by the package authors,
    of the seven McClelland-Elman (1986) feature dimensions, used here as a
    similarity metric only, or from user-supplied similarities. Includes
    model-comparison tools that fit simulated curves to time-course data from
    mouse-tracking, eye-tracking, ERP, or any paradigm producing a
    time-varying competition measure (least-squares scaling and an estimated
    response latency; joint fits of both competition components; a
    single-bump reference), and ships real mouse-tracking data from Lin, Lin, and
    Yeh (2023) for the bundled English and Mandarin stimulus sets.
